 ## Subtopics
 
 ### [Decomposed Spatiotemporal Representation](7525-decomposed-spatiotemporal-representation.md)
-Decomposed Spatiotemporal Representation models dynamic scenes by categorizing volumetric areas into static, deforming, and new content, assigning each category a specialized representation field to optimize for temporal consistency and computational efficiency. It differs from the parent DyNeRF method, which uses a single monolithic MLP conditioned on time, by explicitly separating the scene into distinct temporal behaviors, allowing static regions to remain time-invariant and deforming regions to use canonical space mappings. It is chosen when temporal consistency and computational efficiency across varying scene dynamics are prioritized over a simple baseline implementation.
+Decomposed Spatiotemporal Representation is a method for modeling dynamic volumetric scenes by categorizing spatial regions into static, deforming, and new content, assigning each category a specialized neural field to optimize temporal consistency and computational efficiency. It sets itself apart from the parent DyNeRF method by replacing a single monolithic MLP with a mixture of specialized fields (stationary, deformation, and newness) weighted by a learned decomposition probability, rather than relying on a single temporal embedding.
 
 ### FDRF
-FDRF uses time-aware voxel features to represent scenes and simulates rough motion trajectories with a small coordinate deformation network [S2]. It enhances time information in the radiation network and significantly accelerates the optimization of dynamic radiation fields while maintaining high rendering quality [S2]. It applies a multi-distance interpolation method to voxel features to simulate both small and large motions [S2]. It is chosen when explicit voxel-based acceleration and multi-scale motion simulation are required over a dense MLP representation [S2].
+FDRF is a method that uses time-aware voxel features to represent scenes and simulates rough motion trajectories with a small coordinate deformation network [S2]. It enhances time information in the radiation network and significantly accelerates the optimization of dynamic radiation fields while maintaining high rendering quality [S2].
 
 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Performance:** On the T-LESS dataset, DyNeRF achieves a PSNR of 30.87, SSIM of 0.943, and LPIPS of 0.118 [S1].
-*   **Speed:** Rendering time is 5195 milliseconds [S1].
-*   **Storage:** Storage requirement is 239 MB [S1].
-*   **Strengths:** Simple implementation as a direct extension of static NeRF [S1].
-*   **Limitations:** Loses details in high-frequency regions due to the single shared MLP structure [S1]. Rendering time is significantly higher than some other methods (e.g., 5195 ms vs. 73 ms for NV, though NV has lower quality) [S1].
-*   **When to use:** As a baseline for comparison in dynamic scene reconstruction [S1].
-*   **When not to use:** When high-frequency detail preservation is critical or when computational efficiency is a primary concern [S1].
+*   **Strengths:** Provides a baseline for dynamic NeRF implementation [S1].
+*   **Limitations:** Tends to lose details in high-frequency regions of the image due to its single shared MLP model structure [S1]. Limited by low volume resolution in related baseline comparisons (e.g., Neural Volumes gives blurry results, though this is attributed to resolution, the structural limitation of DyNeRF is the shared MLP) [S1].
